in @glass

# Property: every dissonance value emitted by a fracture body must
# declare a `partials` count equal to the number of distinct AST nodes
# its companion `content: splinter(ast)` actually rewrites.
#
# FOURTH INSTANCE of bilateral pattern #53 (property/fracture; promoted
# 2026-06-10). First instance: keyword/depth
# (@epistemologic/property/keyword_matches_depth +
# @kintsugi/fracture/keyword; 2026-06-10, 5e68df9 + d908798). Second
# instance: gate/diff-closure
# (@epistemologic/property/gate_matches_diff_closure +
# @kintsugi/fracture/gate; 2026-06-10, b8755cc + 20c5d97). Third
# instance: symbol/canonical-form
# (@epistemologic/property/symbol_canonical_form +
# @kintsugi/fracture/symbol_lift; 2026-06-11). This shard + its sibling
# fracture body (@kintsugi/fracture/partials_align) are the FOURTH
# instance, landing at the META-FRACTURE altitude: the bilateral
# pattern applied to its own internal field.
#
# === Seam's 2026-06-11 finding (the load-bearing reading) ===
#
# `partials: 1` looks like template adherence across the bilateral
# cascade. Both `@kintsugi/fracture/keyword` and
# `@kintsugi/fracture/symbol_lift` set `score.partials: 1`. For
# `keyword` (one token swap, one AST node rewritten) the declaration
# is honest. For `symbol_lift` the table mostly contains pure renames
# (one node) but `weighted -> amplitudes` elaborates the AST node into
# a wider record where intensity fields {p_i} lift to complex-amplitude
# pairs {|a_i|, phi_i} — MULTIPLE AST nodes touched per opacity. The
# template-copied `partials: 1` is dishonest for that entry. The
# `partials` field of `dissonance` measures the partial count summed
# in the Helmholtz/Plomp-Levelt roughness curve
# (epistemologic/math/music/dissonance.mirror lines ~219-237); for
# fracture-body-emitted morphisms it MUST equal the breadth of AST
# nodes the morphism's `content: splinter(ast)` discharges. Otherwise
# the substrate lies about dissonance breadth at every altitude — and
# the lie propagates to every future bilateral instance that copies
# the template.
#
# === The discipline catching its own internal field ===
#
# This property is the bilateral pattern self-applying one altitude
# deeper. Every prior instance (#53 first-second-third) declared a
# property about substrate sources OUTSIDE the bilateral chain.
# This instance declares a property about the BILATERAL CHAIN ITSELF
# — specifically about the morphisms fracture bodies emit. The
# substrate's auto-formatter floor self-applies; the discriminator
# discriminates against its own template adherence.
#
# Per recognition #58 (substrate-already-had-the-word — 53rd instance,
# alignment as boundary mathematics at @io; promoted 2026-06-10): the
# alignment harness fires at the substance boundary. Fracture-body-
# emitted morphisms ARE substance crossings — the substrate emits them
# into the kintsugi loop's `active_pass`; they cross from form-side
# (the fracture body's declarative AST) to process-side (the morphism
# the loop applies). This property IS the boundary harness for
# `partials` honesty at that crossing.
#
# === Parametric over fracture-body sites ===
#
# The predicate is parametric over the set of fracture-body sites in
# the substrate. Each opacity surfaces ONE (location, declared_partials,
# computed_breadth) triple — one offending fracture body at one
# `partials` literal site, paired with the honest breadth its
# splinter(ast) discharges. The fracture body resolves any opacity by
# substituting the literal; no per-site variant.
#
# Structurally parallel to symbol_canonical_form (the third instance):
# the predicate is parametric over a computable function (canonical_form
# table lookup there; AST-breadth computation here). The bilateral
# pattern absorbs parametric-over-table AND parametric-over-computation
# variants under the same `@epistemologic/property/<X>` +
# `@kintsugi/fracture/<X>` signature.
#
# === The AST-breadth computation ===
#
# `ast_breadth(content: splinter(ast)) -> u32` reads the content-
# addressed AST fragment and counts the number of distinct nodes the
# splinter rewrites against its source. The substrate already has the
# vocabulary:
#
#   - `splinter(ast)` is declared at glass.mirror lines 261-265 with
#     `content: oid`, `ast: ref`, `transparency: transparency(ast)`.
#     The `content: oid` is the content-addressed handle to the
#     rewritten AST fragment.
#   - The substrate's @meta/ast altitude (boot/01-meta.mirror's
#     `type ast(g)`) names the AST node algebra; the breadth is a
#     traversal cardinality at that altitude.
#   - Per [[architecture-splinter-ast-quote-primitive]] (recognition
#     #54; landed 2026-06-10 at mirror a3789c2): splinter(ast) is the
#     substrate's parametric quote primitive; `ast_breadth` is the
#     dual traversal — quote constructs an AST fragment; ast_breadth
#     measures the cardinality of the construction.
#
# The action stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation reads the
# @meta/ast altitude's node count for the splinter; the substrate
# declares the carrier shape; the arithmetic lands when the kintsugi
# loop pulls.
#
# === Verdict surface ===
#
# Verdict surface is transparency<p>:
#   success              -- every fracture-body-emitted morphism's
#                          `score.partials` literal equals the
#                          ast_breadth of its companion `content:
#                          splinter(ast)`.
#   failure(opacity_map) -- some fracture body declares `partials: N`
#                          where `ast_breadth(content)` returns M with
#                          N != M. Each opacity names ONE (location,
#                          declared_partials, computed_breadth) triple.
#   partial(opacity_map) -- multiple fracture-body sites surveyed, some
#                          honest and some not.
#
# Located transparency (not @glass.verdict): the kintsugi loop
# consumes the opacity_map to direct mutation. Each opacity names ONE
# offending `partials` literal site; the fracture body
# (@kintsugi/fracture/partials_align) realises the rule by lifting
# violations through kintsugi. The property names the rule; the
# fracture realises it; the kintsugi loop is the bridge.
#
# === The recursive structure (Seam's finding catching itself) ===
#
# This property checks fracture-body-emitted morphisms. The kintsugi
# loop's fracture bodies ARE the things being checked. The sibling
# fracture body (@kintsugi/fracture/partials_align) itself emits a
# morphism with a `score.partials` field — which this property will
# inspect. The recursion does not create circularity at runtime:
#
#   - This property reads CURRENT fracture-body source (the .mirror
#     shards declaring `score.partials: N` literals); it does NOT read
#     the kintsugi loop's runtime state.
#   - The fracture body @kintsugi/fracture/partials_align rewrites the
#     OFFENDING `partials` literal at the source level; the loop
#     applies the morphism through `query_phi` like any other.
#   - The fracture body's OWN emitted morphism is one AST-node rewrite
#     (the offending integer literal swap); its honest `score.partials`
#     is `1`. The discipline self-applies: at landing time, the
#     fracture body is declared with `partials: 1` and that declaration
#     passes this property by construction.
#
# Per [[architecture-bateson-form-behaviour-partition]] (recognition
# #50, promoted): this is form-side discipline catching form-side
# template adherence. The form/behaviour partition does not collapse;
# this property and its fracture body sit at form altitude (declared
# rules; declared rewrites); the runtime kintsugi loop applies them at
# process altitude.
#
# === Cybernetic ancestor ===
#
# Recognition #37 (`requires` IS a Paskian agreement; promoted
# 2026-06-10) names `pact` as the property-declaration form. pact
# replaces the conversation-era `grammar` keyword as the surface for
# declaring properties; the rule names `pact` directly below.
#
# Engineering ancestor: every linter that catches its own rules
# (eslint's `no-restricted-syntax` self-applied to lint plugins;
# rustfmt formatting its own source; black's bootstrap) names this
# discipline implicitly. The substrate's contribution is *typing* the
# self-application so the kintsugi loop reads it directly — the
# meta-fracture is a fracture body like any other, not a special case.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   partial count       Helmholtz (1863) Die Lehre von den
#                       Tonempfindungen; the Plomp & Levelt (1965)
#                       Tonal consonance and critical bandwidth — the
#                       `partials` field of a roughness measurement
#                       counts the partials summed when computing the
#                       curve.
#   AST breadth         McCarthy (1960) Recursive functions of
#                       symbolic expressions; the cardinality of
#                       distinct nodes in an S-expression — the
#                       substrate's AST altitude is the M-expression /
#                       S-expression duality at @meta/ast.
#   meta-circularity    Reynolds (1972) Definitional interpreters; the
#                       discipline of a system describing its own
#                       evaluation. The substrate's reading is Smith
#                       (1984) Reflection and semantics in Lisp — the
#                       reflective tower opens a meta-altitude where
#                       the system inspects itself. This property is
#                       the meta-altitude reading of the bilateral
#                       pattern.
#
# The substrate's contribution is *typing* the self-applied check so
# the kintsugi loop reads it. No invention.
#
# Spec context: candidate #53 fourth instance (the bilateral pattern
#               applied to its own internal field; meta-fracture
#               altitude). Recognition #51 (mirror as expanding
#               Hilbert space; promoted) — fracture bodies are how the
#               substrate maintains coherence at each Bateson level;
#               this property maintains coherence at the META-FRACTURE
#               level where the bilateral pattern itself is checked.
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]
#                      (opacity / transparency are substrate vocabulary;
#                      the predicate emits typed carriers throughout),
#                      [[architecture-property-fracture-bilateral]]
#                      (the bilateral pattern this is the fourth
#                      instance of; meta-application altitude),
#                      [[architecture-splinter-ast-quote-primitive]]
#                      (the substrate's parametric AST-fragment
#                      construction; `ast_breadth` is the dual
#                      cardinality traversal of `splinter(ast)`),
#                      [[architecture-alignment-as-boundary-mathematics]]
#                      (recognition #58; this property IS the boundary
#                      harness for `partials` honesty at the
#                      fracture-body-to-morphism crossing),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (opacity is the
#                      typed carrier; no bare (location, N, M) tuple
#                      escapes).
# Forward-promise to consumers: `@kintsugi/oscillate`'s `active_pass`
#                  reads this property's transparency when composing the
#                  per-pulse candidate morphism_set; the binding lands
#                  when the kintsugi loop substrate-pulls. This shard
#                  does NOT pull `@kintsugi/oscillate` or
#                  `@kintsugi/fracture/partials_align`; the substrate-
#                  altitude arrow points upward (the loop and the
#                  fracture body consume this property; not the other
#                  way around).
# Related shards: kintsugi/fracture/partials_align.mirror (the
#                 sibling fracture body; the operational half of this
#                 bilateral instance),
#                 epistemologic/property/keyword_matches_depth.mirror
#                 + kintsugi/fracture/keyword.mirror (FIRST INSTANCE),
#                 epistemologic/property/gate_matches_diff_closure.mirror
#                 + kintsugi/fracture/gate.mirror (SECOND INSTANCE),
#                 epistemologic/property/symbol_canonical_form.mirror
#                 + kintsugi/fracture/symbol_lift.mirror (THIRD
#                 INSTANCE — Seam's finding originates here at the
#                 `weighted -> amplitudes` entry's `partials: 1`),
#                 epistemologic/math/music/dissonance.mirror (where
#                 `dissonance.partials: u32` is declared as the
#                 Plomp-Levelt partial count; this property pins its
#                 fracture-body-emitted value to AST breadth),
#                 glass.mirror (the substrate floor; `opacity`,
#                 `location`, `transparency`, `splinter(ast)`).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/epistemologic/property/dissonance_partials_match_ast_breadth.mirror`
# declares `@epistemologic/property/dissonance_partials_match_ast_breadth`
# as a species under the @epistemologic/property family.
# Keyword/depth property: this declaration is depth-2 from the
# @epistemologic family root; per recognition #46 it uses `pact` (the
# property-declaration form; recognition #37 promoted 2026-06-10).

pact @epistemologic/property/dissonance_partials_match_ast_breadth {
  # The breadth — the count of distinct AST nodes the splinter(ast)
  # actually rewrites against its source. For a pure-rename splinter
  # (one token swap), breadth = 1. For an elaboration that touches
  # multiple AST node fields (e.g. `weighted { p_0, p_1 }` lifting to
  # `amplitudes { a_0, phi_0, a_1, phi_1 }`), breadth = N where N is
  # the count of distinct node changes.
  ast_breadth(content: splinter(ast)) -> u32 { \ }

  # The declared `score.partials` literals observed in fracture-body
  # source. Each entry names ONE `partials: N` literal site inside a
  # fracture-body-emitted morphism; paired with the splinter(ast) in
  # the same morphism whose breadth would supersede the declaration
  # if mismatched.
  fracture_partials_sites(file: ref) -> [opacity] { \ }

  # The combined property: every fracture-body-emitted morphism in
  # `file` has `score.partials` equal to `ast_breadth(content)` of
  # its companion `content: splinter(ast)`.
  # Verdict surface is transparency<p>:
  #   success              -- every fracture-body-emitted morphism's
  #                          `score.partials` literal equals the
  #                          ast_breadth of its companion `content:
  #                          splinter(ast)`.
  #   failure(opacity_map) -- some fracture body declares `partials: N`
  #                          where `ast_breadth(content)` returns M
  #                          with N != M; each opacity names ONE
  #                          (location, declared_partials,
  #                          computed_breadth) triple.
  #   partial(opacity_map) -- multiple fracture-body sites, mixed
  #                          verdict.
  dissonance_partials_match_ast_breadth(file: ref) -> transparency { \ }
}

out ast_breadth
out fracture_partials_sites
out dissonance_partials_match_ast_breadth
out @epistemologic/property/dissonance_partials_match_ast_breadth
